  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to $y=2x-3?$\n\nI. $(-2,-7)$\n\nII. $(0,3)$\n\nIII. $(2,4)$",
  "worked_solution": "To check whether each point is a solution to the equation, we substitute it into the equation and see if it results in a true statement.\n\nFirst, we substitute $(-2,-7)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (-7) & =2(-2)-3 \\\\ -7 & =-4-3 \\\\ -7 & =-7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a true statement, so $(-2,-7)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nNext, we substitute $(0,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (3) & =2(0)-3 \\\\ 3 & =-3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a false statement, so $(0,3)$ is *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nFinally, we substitute $(2,4)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (4) & =2(2)-3 \\\\ 4 & =4-3 \\\\ 4 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is again a false statement, so $(2,4)$ is also *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nSo, out of the three ordered pairs given, the only pair that is a solution to the equation is I $(-2,-7).$",
